2. Inquiry: String Immutability & Slicing Mechanics
Why strings are immutable: Python stores strings as contiguous arrays of Unicode code points.
Immutability guarantees hash stability for dictionary/set keys, enables safe string interning across
variables, and prevents accidental side-effect mutations.
Why text.upper() does not modify text: Because strings cannot change in place, .upper()
allocates a brand-new string object in memory and returns its reference, leaving the original object
intact unless reassigned.
Slicing s[-4:] and s[4:-4]: For a 9-character plate ("KA01M0007"), s[-4:] extracts the last 4
digits ("0007"), while s[4:-4] slices from index 4 up to index 5 (9 - 4), isolating the 1-letter
series ("M"). For a 10-character plate ("TN09AB1234"), s[-4:] extracts "1234" and s[4:-4]
slices indices 4 to 6 (10 - 4), isolating the 2-letter series ("AB").
