2. Inquiry & Alternative Memory/Time Analysis
Why 
join() remains essential despite CPython optimisations: Copying 1 + 2 + … + n characters
yields O(n2) character copies. CPython mitigates this via an in-place 
resize optimization only
when the target string has a reference count of exactly 1. If any secondary variable aliases the string
(e.g., logging or caching intermediate progress), the reference count exceeds 1, disabling in-place
resizing and causing quadratic slowdowns. Moreover, non-CPython runtimes (PyPy, MicroPython)
lack this optimization. 
"".join() guarantees O(n) linear performance everywhere.
Alternative Approaches: Building via 
io.StringIO runs in O(n) time and uses O(n) memory
without creating an intermediate list of fragments. Streaming lines directly to disk with
file.write() achieves O(n) time while consuming only O(1) RAM (a fixed OS write buffer),
making it superior for multi-gigabyte statements.
