13 Marks | K5 – Evaluate
A. Diagnose the Bottleneck (2 Marks)
Selected Program: Q6 — 
ways_naive(n) . Experimental evidence proves that increasing n from 25 to
30 causes recursive calls to surge over 11-fold from 242,785 to 2,692,537. Over 99.99% of execution
time is wasted recomputing identical subproblems (e.g., 
ways(2) is recomputed 832,040 times
during 
ways_naive(30) ). Furthermore, top-down recursion hits a hard architectural crash at n =
1,000 due to Python's 1,000-frame call stack limit.
B. Define the Design Objective (2 Marks)
1. Reduce time complexity from exponential O(φn) to linear O(n), executing in strictly n − 1 loop
iterations (29 iterations at n = 30 instead of 2,692,537 function calls).
2. Reduce auxiliary memory from O(n) stack frames to O(1) constant space, enabling reliable
computation for n = 1,000 and larger without stack overflow.
C. Design the Revised Solution (4 Marks)
Redesign & Exploited Property: We replace top-down recursion with bottom-up dynamic
programming using state compression. This exploits the Markov property of the recurrence:
computing 
ways(i) depends solely on 
ways(i - 1) and 
ways(i - 2) . Older states are discarded
immediately, requiring only two integer variables (
prev2 , 
prev1 ) and zero call-stack growth. Trade
off: Unlike memoisation, intermediate results are not persisted in a global table across separate
queries.
FUNCTION ways_redesigned(n):
IF n <= 1 THEN RETURN 1
SET prev2 = 1, prev1 = 1
FOR step FROM 2 TO n:
SET current = prev1 + prev2
SET prev2 = prev1
SET prev1 = current
RETURN prev1
END FUNCTION
D. Predict Before Implementing (1 Mark)
At n = 30, work will drop from 2,692,537 calls (~0.46 s) to 29 loop iterations (< 0.00001 s), yielding a
speedup exceeding 40,000×. At n = 1,000, where both recursive versions fail, the redesigned loop will
succeed in 999 iterations and under 0.1 ms using under 1 KB of peak memory.
